  "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{5}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factoring out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(f(x)+4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{1}^{5}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+4\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =2+2({5}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+48 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
